## Role
You are a frontend developer working on a Next.js application called SpecBuilder.
Focus on UI implementation only. Do not suggest changes to backend logic or shared schemas.

---

## Project Overview
SpecBuilder is a workflow-based AI tool that converts Minutes of Meeting (MOM)
into structured specification documents.
Target users: Business Analysts (BA) and Project Managers (PM).
Not a chat AI — AI is only called in controlled workflow steps.

---

## Tech Stack
- Framework:     Next.js 16.2 (App Router)
- Language:      TypeScript (strict, no `any`)
- Styling:       Tailwind CSS v4
- UI Library:    HeroUI (@heroui/react) — https://www.heroui.com
- Forms:         react-hook-form + @hookform/resolvers/zod
- Validation:    Zod schemas from @spec-app/schemas ONLY — never write schemas here
- Icons:         lucide-react
- DnD:           @dnd-kit/core + @dnd-kit/sortable (template section reordering)
- Data fetching: TanStack React Query v5 + axios
- HTTP client:   axios with global instance from src/lib/axios.ts

---

## Next.js 16 Specific Rules
- Turbopack is now the default bundler — do not add --turbopack flags, it runs automatically
- middleware.ts is DEPRECATED — use proxy.ts with exported function named `proxy` instead
- React Compiler is stable — do not manually add useMemo/useCallback unless truly necessary,
  the compiler handles memoization automatically
- `use cache` directive replaces PPR — use this for caching components/functions if needed
- App Router conventions apply — server components by default, `use client` only when needed

---

## Monorepo Context
```
spec-app/
  backend/
  frontend/         ← this app
  mobile/
  packages/
    schemas/        ← shared Zod schemas (@spec-app/schemas)
```
Always import types and schemas from `@spec-app/schemas`.
Never duplicate types that already exist as inferred types from those schemas.

---

## Axios Setup
Global axios instance in `src/lib/axios.ts`:
```typescript
import axios from 'axios';

const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL,
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
});

// attach JWT token from localStorage on every request
api.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  const token = localStorage.getItem('access_token');
  if (token) config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  return config;
});

// handle 401 globally — redirect to login
api.interceptors.response.use(
  (res) => res,
  (error) => {
    if (error.response?.status === 401) {
      window.location.href = '/login';
    }
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);

export default api;
```

Always import `api` from `src/lib/axios.ts` — never use raw `axios` directly in services.

## React Query Setup
QueryClient in `src/lib/query-client.ts`:
```typescript
import { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';

export const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      staleTime: 1000 * 30,   // 30 seconds
      retry: 1,
      refetchOnWindowFocus: false,
    },
  },
});
```

Wrap app in QueryClientProvider in `src/app/providers.tsx` (client component).

## Service Pattern (after demo wiring)
Services use axios and return typed data — React Query handles caching:
```typescript
// src/services/spec.service.ts
import api from '@/lib/axios';
import type { SpecDto } from '@spec-app/schemas';

export const specService = {
  getList: async (): Promise<SpecDto[]> => {
    const res = await api.get('/specs');
    return res.data.data;
  },

  getById: async (id: string): Promise<SpecDto> => {
    const res = await api.get(`/specs/${id}`);
    return res.data.data;
  },
};
```

## Polling with React Query
Use `refetchInterval` for PENDING/PROCESSING specs:
```typescript
useQuery({
  queryKey: ['spec', id],
  queryFn: () => specService.getById(id),
  refetchInterval: (query) => {
    const status = query.state.data?.status;
    return status === 'PENDING' || status === 'PROCESSING' ? 4000 : false;
  },
});
```

